WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:05.321
The quality of an actor's work on-screen might
be a matter of opinion most of the time, but

00:00:05.321 --> 00:00:10.460
there are some performances so awful that
all subjectivity goes out the window.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:15.360
In the case of these scenes, the acting is
so over-the-top and bizarre that it's hard

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.699
not to laugh at them, even years after the
fact.

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:20.980
Talk about sucking

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.659
Just about everyone involved in the adaptation
of Stephenie Meyer’s vampire romance could

00:00:25.659 --> 00:00:31.749
be accused of phoning it in, but Twilight’s
MVP Kristen Stewart takes top honors for worst

00:00:31.749 --> 00:00:34.160
performance in the movie's climactic scene.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.500
It all starts when a trio of nomadic vamps
lurk around, looking for something to eat

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.850
when they stumble across the Cullen clan playing
baseball while their protected human pet watches

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:45.850
on.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:51.100
A standoff ensues and almost ends peacefully
— before James catches a whiff of Bella

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.640
gets the munchies.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.320
"You brought a snack."

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.980
The situation is diffused, but not done.

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:05.200
James, the tracker vampire, vows to hunt her
down and eat her.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.300
Despite the Cullens' best efforts to hide
her, he lures her away from the Cullens and

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.430
savagely snaps her leg.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:16.660
Bella's sparkly vampire boyfriend Edward arrives
to save her from certain death, but James

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.520
still manages to sink his teeth into her arm.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.410
The venom from his bite starts to spread through
her veins right away, which is when she informed

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.410
everyone:

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.810
"Oh, no, no, no, my hand is BURNING!

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.500
In Meyer’s book, the pain is described as,
quote, "exquisite," but watching Stewart’s

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:37.750
grunting and grimacing in the moment makes
those tiny teeth marks look anything but.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.360
The nose knows

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.020
Richard Linklater’s coming-of-age classic
Dazed and Confused gave us the gift of many

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.920
quotable Matthew McConaughey moments …

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.500
"You just gotta keep livin' man.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.770
L-I-V-I-N."

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:55.110
But it's also responsible for the unbearable
nuisance of Mitch Kramer.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:59.790
The goober freshman is a categorical case
study in social awkwardness, so there's some

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.270
lack of screen savvy to be expected from his
portrayal.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.950
But in the scene where he’s trying to flirt
with Sabrina outside the Emporium, it's full-tilt

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.160
amateur hour.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.880
Instead of coming out with traditional acting
devices like facial expressions, vocal inflection,

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:20.040
or pure presence, actor Wiley Wiggins relied
on eight — yes eight — cringe-worthy nose

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.280
...and one super clumsy elbow nudge to coast
through the scene.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.370
Linklater eventually accepted responsibility
for letting this bad bit slip through the

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:48.490
editing racks, telling the Daily Beast, "I
just thought [the nose grab] was kind of an

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:49.490
awkward gesture.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.010
I’m the director, so it’s on me!

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.360
That’s a thing people only notice if it’s
the third time you watch it.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.680
I just thought it was that young, awkward
man kind of thing.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.069
Maybe it was one or two too many."

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:06.440
When it comes to removing a few of those nose-grabs,
maybe on a remastered cut of the movie, well…

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.180
"It'd be a lot cooler if you did."

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.620
Pass the turkey

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:17.530
Considering Gigli is a movie whose 6% Rotten
Tomatoes score is probably a little generous,

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.340
there are a lot of awful aspects to choose
from.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.340
"You know something?

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.340
You're right.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:23.340
It's sad.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.420
It's sadness, and I'm f---ing sad."

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:30.040
The stupidity of this thing extends all the
way up to the title itself, so it’s a tall

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.030
order to pick just one scene as the worst
of the worst.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:39.140
Since Gigli was such a turkey at the box office,
though, it makes sense to focus on the scene

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:44.850
that tried — and failed, miserably — to
make Thanksgiving-themed cuisine a workable

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.160
piece of sexy dialogue.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.500
In the movie, Jennifer Lopez plays a lesbian
named Ricki who decides to make a Ben Affleck's

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.240
hitman her boytoy and invites him into her
bed.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.440
Like so:

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:57.960
"It's turkey time …"

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:00.620
"Huh?"

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.300
"Gobble gobble."

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.660
That's when everyone watching this movie did
this:

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.060
The turkey talk is an evergreen highlight
for both Ben and Jen's career blooper reels,

00:04:17.069 --> 00:04:20.219
even though they've tried to put the picture
into perspective ever since.

00:04:20.219 --> 00:04:23.279
"There's worse movies than Gigli out there."

00:04:23.279 --> 00:04:24.279
The empty box

00:04:24.279 --> 00:04:30.169
With all due respect to Brad Pitt, his performance
in Seven is a classic case of an actor completely

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:33.529
missing the emotional mark of an important
movie moment.

00:04:33.529 --> 00:04:38.169
Instead of delivering on what might've been
a powerful scene, he made a bit of a mockery

00:04:38.169 --> 00:04:39.289
of himself.

00:04:39.289 --> 00:04:44.300
To be fair to Pitt, he was working with three
industry giants — director David Fincher

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.360
and actors Kevin Spacey and Morgan Freeman
— while he was still something of a Hollywood

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.180
newcomer at the time.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.500
But compared to everyone else’s delivery
in the "wrath" scene, his contribution is

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.099
pretty inexcusable.

00:04:56.099 --> 00:05:00.610
In the grand finale of John Doe’s deadly
sins-inspired plan, Kevin Spacey’s killer

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:07.069
character leads the detective duo out to the
desert to find a package containing … something.

00:05:07.069 --> 00:05:11.759
Freeman's character gets a gander at it first
and his face dutifully reflects the grim reality

00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:13.439
of the situation.

00:05:13.439 --> 00:05:18.060
We're led to believe it’s the head of Mills’
pregnant wife Tracy, an innocent and lovely

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:23.660
lady who has nothing to do with any of this
mess, but we never get to see the contents.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:28.599
The tension is staggering...until Pitt starts
screaming his lines and jumping around like

00:05:28.599 --> 00:05:29.599
a carnival clown.

00:05:29.599 --> 00:05:32.330
"Show you what was in the box what was in
the boxxxx?"

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:35.349
"AHHH what's in the boxxxxxx" through "what's
in the f---in' box!"

00:05:35.349 --> 00:05:37.999
It’s a scene-ruiner upon rewatching it.

00:05:37.999 --> 00:05:42.620
Looking back, you might be tempted to believe
it was secretly Pitt’s talent that was actually

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.610
hidden in the box.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.300
Those bloody wolves

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:51.069
If Keanu Reeves could really take a Bill and
Ted-style journey back in time, we hope he’d

00:05:51.069 --> 00:05:56.199
at least consider returning to the early '90s
and undoing everything he did in Bram Stoker’s

00:05:56.199 --> 00:05:57.199
Dracula.

00:05:57.199 --> 00:06:01.539
At the very least, he should retroactively
hire himself a proper dialect coach because

00:06:01.539 --> 00:06:06.550
his British accent is so horrible that it
actually distracts viewers from fixating on

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:10.159
Gary Oldman’s giant butt wig, which is quite
a feat.

00:06:10.159 --> 00:06:15.969
In one scene in particular, Reeves’ character
starts to realize that the Count is bad news

00:06:15.969 --> 00:06:21.009
and delivers a recap of his Transylvanian
adventures that's way funnier than it's supposed

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:22.009
to be.

00:06:22.009 --> 00:06:27.740
"I’ve seen many strange things already—bloody
wolves chasing me through some blue inferno."

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:32.439
Even director Francis Ford Coppola, who’s
practically president of the Keanu Reeves

00:06:32.439 --> 00:06:37.119
Fan Club, had to admit to Entertainment Weekly,
"it was tough for him to affect an English

00:06:37.119 --> 00:06:38.219
accent.

00:06:38.219 --> 00:06:39.339
He tried so hard.

00:06:39.339 --> 00:06:43.969
That was the problem, actually — he wanted
to do it perfectly and in trying to do it

00:06:43.969 --> 00:06:46.559
perfectly, it came off as stilted."

00:06:46.559 --> 00:06:47.559
In other words ...

00:06:47.559 --> 00:06:51.529
"I doubted everything even my mind.

00:06:51.529 --> 00:06:54.699
I was impotent with fear."

00:06:54.699 --> 00:06:55.699
The fumble

00:06:55.699 --> 00:07:01.779
1999 presented two major movie moments depicting
sons rejecting their stodgy old dads’ wishes

00:07:01.779 --> 00:07:04.379
for them to follow in their footsteps.

00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:07.190
And one of those things was not like the other.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.960
Jake Gyllenhaal’s breakthrough performance
as Homer Hickam in October Sky features him

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:16.800
standing up to the status quo of going to
the mine shaft after school with his dad.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.580
Instead he literally reaches for the sky as
a wannabe space cadet who built rockets on

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:22.580
the sly.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:26.780
It builds to a poignant moment of father-son
friction that totally resonates the way it

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:27.780
should.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.580
"Coal-mining's your life.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.040
It's not mine."

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:42.360
Compare that to James Van Der Beek’s showdown
with his own pop in Varsity Blues, which hit

00:07:42.379 --> 00:07:44.409
theaters a few weeks before.

00:07:44.409 --> 00:07:48.990
In the movie, Van Der Beek plays a backup
high school quarterback named Mox who's hiked

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:53.870
his way to the starting squad after an injury
takes out the team’s original star.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:59.120
Somehow this ignites some long-held dream
his dad has for Mox to become the star player.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.939
But Mox has no such ambitions and tells him
as much… in the most exaggerated and terrible

00:08:04.939 --> 00:08:05.939
accent imaginable.

00:08:05.939 --> 00:08:10.770
"Playing football in West Canaan may have
been the opportunity of your lifetime, but

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.289
I don't want your life."

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:16.800
It's hard to tell whether it's the dull delivery
tone or the lines themselves that make this

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.029
such a dud, but he gets no points for that
scene.

00:08:21.029 --> 00:08:22.379
Green bobblin'

00:08:22.379 --> 00:08:26.960
James Franco made a jump to the blockbuster
A-list with Sam Raimi’s Spider-Man trilogy,

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.529
but it’s also a regular go-to source for
some of his most accidentally lousy acting

00:08:32.529 --> 00:08:33.529
moments.

00:08:33.529 --> 00:08:38.270
His character spends the first sequel desperately
seeking the web-slinging vigilante who he

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:40.870
believes killed his dad in the first film.

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:46.130
And when he lifts the mask to reveal Spidey's
identity, he discovers his best friend Peter

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.980
Parker is his new worst enemy.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:52.460
"Pete…

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:56.640
No, it can't be."

00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:02.320
Franco’s slack-jawed gape and awkward footwork
is only exceeded in badness by his whispered

00:09:02.329 --> 00:09:03.850
threats about getting payback.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:08.730
"If only I could cause you the pain that you've
caused me."

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:13.740
The whole thing adds up to a scene more cartoonish
than the comics it was based on.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.560
Rain rain go away

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:20.740
Andie MacDowell never quite lived up to the
promise she displayed in movies from the late-80s

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:22.050
and early '90s.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:25.430
Maybe it was because of her wooden performance
in Four Weddings and a Funeral.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:29.740
"I don't usually skulk a lot but I suppose
I could skulk if skulking were required."

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:33.850
Although the film is otherwise moderately
charming, there's one scene that tends to

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:35.120
ruin it on every re-watch.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.920
At the end of the movie, in the pouring rain,
Hugh Grant delivers one of those classic,

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:47.500
sappy "It was you all along" speeches to McDowell,
but his monologue is interrupted by one very

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:48.820
pointless observation:

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:50.800
"Is it still raining?

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.000
I hadn't noticed."

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:57.420
There's so little emotion in the moment from
MacDowell that it's almost as if she were

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:00.350
the funeral featured in the film's title.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:01.350
Thanks for watching!

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.920
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.440
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

